Warlord's Lore
The Starchild's Lore
The Starchild was chosen by the stars themselves, her heart bound to the Eternal Core. With its radiance she bore the gifts of her people — shields of dawn, wings of light, and the forbidden art to stir the hearts of suns. To the Sun Civilization she was both guardian and flame eternal, but beneath the divine radiance lived a woman who carried the weight of every choice that shaped the war.
She had always been protective, perhaps too much so. When the Archmage first approached with his scholarly questions and earnest desire to understand the deeper mysteries, she welcomed him into her inner circle despite the warnings from The False Oracle. "Knowledge itself cannot be evil," she would say, her optimism shining as bright as her stellar fire. That trust would become her greatest regret.
The Great Betrayal shattered more than her armies—it broke something fundamental in her worldview. The scholar she had nurtured became the architect of slaughter, her closest friend and protector sacrificed his very identity to save her from a fate worse than death. In the aftermath, as she knelt beside Nightblade's unconscious form and saw the emptiness where recognition should have been, she wept tears of liquid starlight that burned craters into the crystal floor.
When Nightblade returned months later and massacred her people in their moment of joy, she felt the weight of every death as a personal failure. She had watched The Dawnbreaker battle their corrupted champion, had seen the spark of doubt in his movements when confronted with their shared past. Part of her wanted to intervene, to speak the words that might break through his conditioning, but she held back—not from fear, but from the terrible knowledge that her voice might be the trigger that destroyed whatever fragments of the real Nightblade still remained.
"I should have seen it coming," she confided to The Dawnbreaker after his retreat. "I who am supposed to be the light that illuminates all shadows, and I was blind to the darkness growing in my own heart." Her guilt manifests in her relentless pursuit of perfection in battle, her reluctance to delegate crucial decisions, and her tendency to shoulder burdens that should be shared.
Yet for all her self-doubt, The Starchild remains the beacon her people need. Her compassion, though wounded, has not diminished—it has simply become more careful, more aware of the prices that love and trust can demand. She still believes in redemption, still holds hope that the man who gave everything to protect her might one day remember who he chose to be. Until then, she carries the light for all of them, burning ever brighter against the encroaching dark.

The Dawnbreaker's Lore
The Dawnbreaker's path was not born of divine decree but forged in the harsh light of discipline. From the moment she could stand, her body was a canvas for the martial arts, each movement a prayer to the sun. Her peers fell away, unable to match her relentless pursuit of perfection, a mastery so profound that every fiber of her being obeyed her conscious will, and her form became a perfect instrument for her purpose.
Her story of loss began long after the Great Betrayal had ended. Months had passed since Nightblade's disappearance, and the Lightborne mourned their fallen champion. Then, without warning, he returned. The sentries saw his familiar silhouette approaching their gates and opened them with joy—their hero had conquered the Void and come home at last.
The massacre that followed painted the crystal halls red. Nightblade moved through the celebration like a shadow given form, his blade singing its silent song of death. Lightborne fell before they could even comprehend the betrayal, their joy turning to horror as their savior became their destroyer. By the time The Dawnbreaker arrived, dozens lay dead, and Nightblade stood in the center of the carnage like a statue carved from darkness.
Their battle shook the very foundations of the citadel. Her perfect martial forms met his flawless assassin's techniques in a dance of light and shadow. As they fought, she spoke to the fragments of the man she had once called brother-in-arms.
"I know you're still in there," she said between strikes, her luminous blades crossing with his shadowed steel. "This isn't who you are."
His response was silence, but she caught something in his hesitation—a flicker of uncertainty when she spoke of their shared training, their old sparring matches, the oaths they had sworn together.
"You protected her once," The Dawnbreaker pressed, parrying a strike that would have severed her head. "You gave everything to keep The Starchild safe. That sacrifice—that choice—it came from your heart, not from their darkness."
For the briefest moment, Nightblade's assault faltered. Behind his mask, something stirred—confusion, pain, the ghost of recognition. But the moment passed, and his next strike came with renewed fury.
When The Dawnbreaker finally forced him to retreat, bloodied but unbroken, she saw him pause at the threshold. He touched the white sheath at his side with something that might have been reverence, then looked back at her with eyes that seemed almost... questioning.
"Find your way back to us," she whispered, and he vanished into the void.
Now The Dawnbreaker carries the weight of that encounter, knowing that somewhere beneath the Void's perfect weapon lies the soul of a warrior who chose to sacrifice everything for light. She trains harder, pushes herself further, preparing for the day when that choice might resurface—and when it does, she will be ready to stand beside him once again.

Attack Moon's Lore
It is a legend whispered only in the deepest archives of the Sun Civilization, a relic of desperate alliance forged in the fires of the Great Betrayal. The Attack Moon is not a person, but a living construct, a sentient fragment of a star bound in ancient, impenetrable armor. It was built as retaliation, a weapon of such power that it could bring judgment to those who had wounded the light itself.
When the Void Archmage's betrayal devastated the Lightborne forces and claimed Nightblade, The Starchild knew conventional warfare would not suffice. In her darkest hour, she reached out to the enigmatic Paradox Faction, seeking their impossible technology. The Quantum Empress, moved by ancient bonds of alliance, provided the paradoxical engineering needed to create something that existed both as weapon and fortress, both real and impossible.
The Attack Moon's descent upon the Void was like a falling star carrying the wrath of creation itself. Its impossible weapons, forged from both stellar fire and paradoxical physics, brought devastation to the void-touched worlds. Yet even this ultimate weapon could not achieve total victory—the combined might of the Void Archmage, The Null Hour, and their dark legions managed to wound it and force its retreat.
Now the Attack Moon waits in the depths of space, its consciousness holding memories of battles fought across realities, its guns trained on distant stars. It is both monument to alliance between Light and Paradox, and testament to the terrible cost of betrayal. When it next awakens, the very fabric of space will tremble at its judgment.

God Engine Lore:
Solar Colossus  (Sunborn God Machine)
Forged upon the desert forges of Erythra, the Solar Colossus was carved from the buried citadels of glass and bound with the Ember of Dawn. Its core houses the Sunborn’s shard of the Silver Key, which fuels an eternal furnace.
Its mere presence scorches the battlefield; armies ignite like offerings, and night turns to endless noon.
The Necronomium fragment woven into its design describes “The Flame that Burns Beyond Dawn”, and so the Solar Colossus is revered as the Sunborn’s proof of divine succession.
